                <pb facs="0757" n="749" sameAs="frus:frus1939v02/pg749"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.00/8972: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-02-18T12:00:00+00:00">February 18,
                        1939—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received February 18—10:55 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>319. Jules Henry said to me last night that Negrin and Del Vayo were now ready to
                    accept immediate surrender provided Franco would agree that there should not be
                    executions by way of reprisal. Jules Henry said that the latest news from Burgos
                    indicated that Franco would refuse this proviso and insist on immediate
                    unconditional surrender. The French Government believed that Negrin and his
                    supporters in Valencia and Madrid therefore would fight to the bitter end
                    because surrender would mean firing squads.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
